\begin_layout Section
Operators
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
To process information,
 we need a way to transform quantum states
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
To do this,
 we introduce operators
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The general operator 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{O}$
\end_inset

 transforms a quantum state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

 into another state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\chi}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{O}\ket{\psi}=\ket{\chi}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Here,
 operator 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{O}$
\end_inset

 is said to 
\series bold
act
\series default
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection
The NOT operator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Consider a bit flip operator,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{X}$
\end_inset

,
 that transforms a bit 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{x}$
\end_inset

 into its complement,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\bar{x}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Explicitly,
 this is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{X}\ket{0}=\ket{1}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\mathbf{X}\ket{1}=\ket{0}\label{eq:NOTaction}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
In the linear algebraic world,
 a matrix is used to transform vectors,
 just as operators transform kets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If we continue to use the state-to-vector assignments of Equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:ElementaryBasisKets"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 we have chosen to work in the computational basis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Finding the Matrix Representation in a given Basis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Matrix Columns
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Since we've made the state-to-vector assignments as in Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:ElementaryBasisKets"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 we can find the matrix representation of an operator in the basis 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \ket{0},\ket{1}\right\} $
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We may use a fact from linear algebra to find the matrix representation of some operator:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{e_{k}}$
\end_inset

 be the elementary vector of all zeros,
 except for 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-th element,
 which is 1
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Example:
 in three dimensions,
 we can define three elementary vectors:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\vec{e}_{1}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
1\\
0\\
0
\end{array}\right],\qquad\vec{e}_{2}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
0\\
1\\
0
\end{array}\right],\qquad\text{and}\qquad\vec{e}_{3}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
0\\
0\\
1
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We can use 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{e}_{k}$
\end_inset

 to pick out the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-th column of a matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
M\vec{e}_{k}=k\text{-th colum of }M\label{eq:FindMatrixColumn}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Example:
 let
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
M=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
a & b & c\\
d & e & f\\
g & h & i
\end{array}\right]\label{eq:M3x3}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Then:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
M\vec{e}_{1}=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
a & b & c\\
d & e & f\\
g & h & i
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
1\\
0\\
0
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{c}
a\\
d\\
g
\end{array}\right]\quad\left(\text{first column of }M\right),
\]

\end_inset

and we could also show that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
M\vec{e}_{2}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
b\\
e\\
h
\end{array}\right]\quad\left(\text{second col. of }M\right),\quad\text{and}\quad M\vec{e}_{3}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
c\\
f\\
i
\end{array}\right]\quad\left(\text{third col. of }M\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
