\begin_layout Plain Layout

\series bold
Exercise 3.1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Find 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

,
 the matrix representation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{X}$
\end_inset

 in the computational basis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+html:
 <details><summary>Show/hide solution...</summary>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
To do this,
 we can build the matrix column by column:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\mathbf{X}\ket{0} & \mathbf{X}\ket{1}\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\ket{1} & \ket{0}\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0 & 1\\
1 & 0
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+html:
 </details>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+end_note
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Greyedout
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+begin_note
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\series bold
Exercise 3.2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Verify that 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 transforms the basis states consistent with Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:NOTaction"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+html:
 <details><summary>Show/hide solution...</summary>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{X}\ket{0} & =\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0 & 1\\
1 & 0
\end{array}\right]\begin{bmatrix}1\\
0
\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}0\\
1
\end{bmatrix}=\ket{1}\\
\mathbf{X}\ket{1} & =\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0 & 1\\
1 & 0
\end{array}\right]\begin{bmatrix}0\\
1
\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}1\\
0
\end{bmatrix}=\ket{0}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+html:
 </details>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+end_note
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Matrix Elements
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{M}\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

 is a state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\chi}$
\end_inset

,
 then we can take an inner product of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{M}\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

 with another state,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\phi}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{\phi|\mathbf{M}|\psi}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{\phi|\mathbf{M}|\psi}$
\end_inset

 is a scalar,
 we call 
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{\phi|\mathbf{M}|\psi}$
\end_inset

 the 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\phi,\psi\right)$
\end_inset

 matrix element of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{M}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A matrix element 
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{\psi|\mathbf{M}|\psi}$
\end_inset

 is said to be a diagonal element of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{M}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Recall that the trace of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{M}$
\end_inset

 is the sum of diagonal elements
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
If we have an orthonormal basis 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \ket{\psi_{j}}\right\} $
\end_inset

 for the Hilbert space of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{M}$
\end_inset

,
 we can write the trace of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{M}$
\end_inset

 as the sum of the diagonal elements:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\text{Tr}\;\left(\mathbf{M}\right)=\sum_{j}\Braket{\psi_{j}|\mathbf{M}|\psi_{j}}\label{eq:trace}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Interestingly,
 the trace is basis-independent
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We can choose any two orthonormal bases 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \ket{\psi_{j}}\right\} $
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \ket{\psi_{j}^{\prime}}\right\} $
\end_inset

,
 and if we evaluate the trace of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{M}$
\end_inset

 in both bases,
 we get the same number
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
