\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Quantum circuits are represented as timelines that are read from left to right.
 By convention,
 it is assumed that each qubit starts at the left in state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

.
 Blocks or symbols along the timeline indicate qubit operations (quantum gates).
 Typically,
 each qubit gets its own timeline.
 (a) A straight line indicates a single-qubit timeline with no transformations,
 such that an initial 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 is untransformed,
 leading to a final state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

.
 (b) A single-qubit timeline has a NOT gate (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{X}$
\end_inset

) that turns the initial state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 into the final state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{1}$
\end_inset

 .
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:BasicSingleQubitCircuits"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Let's learn about quantum circuits and see what a circuit with a NOT gate looks like
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In quantum circuits,
 each qubit gets its own timeline that we read from left to right
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
By convention,
 it is assumed that the initial state of the qubit is 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Basic single-qubit quantum circuits are shown
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:SingleQbitTimeline"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

:
 A straight line indicates a single-qubit timeline without any transformations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:SingleQbitNotCircuit"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

:
 A single qubit transformed from 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{1}$
\end_inset

 using a NOT operator is drawn as a straight line connecting to boxed 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{X}$
\end_inset

 (NOT gate)
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The gate input is on the left and is the conventional 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The gate output is on the right (final state) and is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{X}\ket{0}=\ket{1}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:SingleQbitBA"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

:
 two operations,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{A}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{B}$
\end_inset

,
 transform the initial state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 in sequence
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{A}$
\end_inset

 is on the left,
 it is applied first to 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

,
 leading to an intermediate state 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{A}\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The intermediate state is transformed by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{B}$
\end_inset

 to the final state,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{A}\ket{0}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Note that the the ordering for the mathematical expression 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{A}\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 is consistent with the linear algebraic representation by a transformation by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{A}$
\end_inset

 first and then 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{B}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In other words,
 the linear algebraic expression is written in sequence from right to left,
 but the circuit transformations are drawn in sequence from left to right
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
