\begin_layout Subsection
The Pauli Operators
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The NOT operator 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{X}$
\end_inset

 is equivalent to the Pauli operator,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{X}=\sigma_{x}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0 & 1\\
1 & 0
\end{array}\right]\label{eq:sigmax}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In addition to 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{x}$
\end_inset

,
 there are two other Pauli operators,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{Y}=\sigma_{y}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0 & i\\
-i & 0
\end{array}\right],\qquad\text{and}\qquad\mathbf{Z}=\sigma_{z}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
-1 & 0\\
0 & 1
\end{array}\right]\label{eq:sigmayz}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Note:
 most often,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{Y}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{Z}$
\end_inset

 will be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{Y}=\sigma_{y}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0 & -i\\
i & 0
\end{array}\right],\qquad\text{and}\qquad\mathbf{Z}=\sigma_{z}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 0\\
0 & -1
\end{array}\right]\label{eq:sigma_y_z_more_common}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
There are two conventions here
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The least popular convention orders the eigenvalues of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{Z}$
\end_inset

 from least to greatest going down the diagonal
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
It is more common in quantum computing literature to use the definitions of Equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:sigma_y_z_more_common"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Thus,
 we will prefer the convention of Equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:sigma_y_z_more_common"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) for quantum computing applications,
 and we may use the convention of Equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:sigmayz"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) when doing quantum mechanics
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
While 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{X}$
\end_inset

 can be understood classically,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{Y}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{Z}$
\end_inset

 do not have a classical meaning
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
These gates introduce a complex phase
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Complex phase is important because it allows for constructive and destructive interference of quantum wave functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In generally,
 we can think of quantum computations as interference experiments:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We set up a quantum circuit to cancel probabilities of unwanted answers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
After measurement,
 we achieve the desired answer,
 or some combination of desirable answers
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
