\begin_layout Plain Layout

\series bold
Exercise 3.5
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
How does 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{Z}$
\end_inset

 transform the states 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{1}$
\end_inset

?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+html:
 <details><summary>Show/hide solution...</summary>
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{Z}\ket{0}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 0\\
0 & -1
\end{array}\right]\begin{bmatrix}1\\
0
\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}1\\
0
\end{bmatrix}=\ket{0}\label{eq:Zket0}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{Z}\ket{1}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 0\\
0 & -1
\end{array}\right]\begin{bmatrix}0\\
1
\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}0\\
-1
\end{bmatrix}=-\ket{1}\label{eq:Zket1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

#+html:
 </details>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+end_note
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Complex Phase and Interference
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
What does it mean for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{Z}$
\end_inset

 to transform 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{1}$
\end_inset

 into 
\begin_inset Formula $-\ket{1}$
\end_inset

?
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The NOT operation (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{X}$
\end_inset

) has classical significance,
 but 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{Z}$
\end_inset

 cannot be understood in a way that is classical
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The negative sign,
 that is,
 a factor of 
\begin_inset Formula $-1=e^{i\pi}$
\end_inset

,
 is said to be a 
\series bold
complex phase factor
\series default
 (also:
 
\series bold
complex phase
\series default
 or 
\series bold
phase factor
\series default
 or 
\series bold
phase
\series default
).
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This has no classical meaning,
 but phase allows for constructive or destructive interference
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsubsection
Interference
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
To understand interference,
 let's introduce another operator,
 the Hadamard transformation,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{H}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{H}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left(\mathbf{X}+\mathbf{Z}\right)\label{eq:defHadamard}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The next exercise will prepare us to discover interference in quantum states.
\end_layout

