\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This is a destructive interference,
 which leads to zero probability 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{k}^{\ast}c_{k}=\left|c_{k}\right|^{2}$
\end_inset

 of measuring certain outcomes
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Similarly,
 we have the constructive interference of certain other probability amplitudes 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{k^{\prime}}$
\end_inset

 which enhances the probability 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{k^{\prime}}^{\ast}c_{k^{\prime}}=\left|c_{k^{\prime}}\right|^{2}$
\end_inset

 of measuring these outcomes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We have now seen constructive and destructive interference at work in a very simple case with quantum information
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
It turns out that interference is one of the key principles in quantum computing
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
quantum circuits use destructive interference to suppress the probability of measuring certain unwanted outcomes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
other more-desirable outcomes have enhanced measurement probabilities via constructive interference
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
measurement is a probabilistic process that returns one of the outcomes with a non-zero probability of measurement
\end_layout

\end_deeper
